findings.body:
The `__str__` method added to the `SubscriptionPlan` TypedDict cannot run. TypedDict instances are plain `dict`s at runtime, and `str()` on a plain dict uses `dict.__repr__`, not the method on the class. I checked this in isolation: `str(SubscriptionPlan(plan="a", expiration_date=1))` returns `{'plan': 'a', 'expiration_date': 1}`.

The method also suggests a debug format that nothing actually produces. Remove it. If a readable format is needed, add a helper function instead.

::diff{file="api/services/billing_service.py" lines="22-30"}

The `setup_redis_cleanup` fixture has a comment `# Clean up before test`, but nothing runs before the `yield`. Only the teardown after `yield` deletes `tenant_plan:*` keys. If a previous run was killed before teardown, stale `tenant-1`/`tenant-2` keys stay in Redis. Several tests then see unexpected cache hits: for example, `test_get_plan_bulk_with_cache_all_cache_miss` expects the billing API to be called, which won't happen with stale data.

::code{file="api/tests/test_containers_integration_tests/services/test_billing_service.py" lines="22-33"}

Run the same deletion before the `yield`, so every test starts from a clean prefix.

The old `get_plan_bulk` put validation inside the chunk-level `try`. One malformed record raised there, so the `except` discarded all 200 tenants in that chunk. The new per-tenant `try` isolates the failure and keeps the other 199 valid plans. The log message names the tenant, so bad data is easy to find.

The mechanism is right. The only problem is what goes into the result for the failing key, covered in the finding above.

